# gentle-ai

[gentle-ai](https://github.com/Gentleman-Programming/gentle-ai) is part of the standard
toolchain on this machine. It adapts the agents you already have — Cursor, Claude Code,
Codex and others — with the persona, Engram memory, the SDD phases, curated skills, and
receipt-driven review.

```bash
gentle-ai update    # version check: gentle-ai, engram, gga
gentle-ai sync      # re-apply managed config at the current version (non-interactive)
gentle-ai install   # first run on a machine; picks persona and preset
```

## Who owns what

This toolkit and gentle-ai write into `~/.cursor/` without overlapping, and the installers
are order-independent. The failure mode when that breaks is silent deletion, not a conflict.

| Path | Owner |
|---|---|
| `~/.cursor/rules/gentle-ai.mdc` | gentle-ai |
| `~/.cursor/rules/toolkit-*.mdc`, `~/.cursor/skills/`, `~/.cursor/loop/` | this toolkit |

In `~/.codex/AGENTS.md` the split is inverted: gentle-ai owns the file, and this
toolkit owns only its `<!-- agent-dotfiles:global -->` fenced region.

## Do

- Read `~/.cursor/rules/gentle-ai.mdc` for the persona and orchestration contract — it is the source, not this file.
- Run `gentle-ai sync` after a version bump, or `gentle-ai update` to check for one.
- Treat `gentle-ai review mode` as the user's switch. It is **off** by default.

## Don't

- Don't hand-edit `gentle-ai.mdc` or any `<!-- gentle-ai:* -->` fenced block. `gentle-ai sync` overwrites both.
- Don't name a toolkit rule `gentle-ai.mdc`, and don't add skills under a `_shared/` directory — those names belong to gentle-ai.
- Don't enable receipt-driven review on the user's behalf, and don't work around it while it is disabled. Deliver under ordinary repository policy instead.
